(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["pharmacology-i-exam-1-pharm-exam-1-master-exam-form-d-v1"]={"title":"Pharmacology I Exam 1 Master Exam (v1, archived) — Form D","category":"Pharmacology I","exam":"Exam 1","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Which systemic effects can topical corticosteroids cause?","choices":["Adrenal suppression, infections, hyperglycaemia, glaucoma, cataracts and growth retardation in children","Hepatotoxicity, nephrotoxicity and ototoxicity","Raised serum lipids and brittle nails","Photosensitivity, severe sunburn, desquamation, burning and stinging that decrease with continued use"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. That a topical agent can do all of this is the point of the potency and duration warnings. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"What is the depot effect, and what does it allow?","choices":["Skin acts as a reservoir for the drug, which may permit once daily dosing of short acting drugs","The drug is stored in subcutaneous fat and released during exercise, which is why activity increases absorption","The vehicle evaporates and leaves crystals that dissolve slowly in sweat","Repeated applications saturate hair follicles and shut down absorption"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The reservoir is what lets a short acting drug be given less often than its half-life suggests. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"Why are norepinephrine's actions diminished but not reversed by phenoxybenzamine?","choices":["Norepinephrine is metabolised faster than epinephrine","Norepinephrine acts only on dopaminergic receptors","It lacks significant beta activity on vessels","Norepinephrine is not bound by alpha receptors"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, so there is no vasodilating component left to unmask. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 109"},{"q":"Which two alpha-one blockers are used for benign prostatic hyperplasia?","choices":["Labetalol and carvedilol","Tamsulosin and alfuzosin","Prazosin and terazosin","Phentolamine and phenoxybenzamine"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, while prazosin, terazosin and doxazosin are listed for hypertension. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 111"},{"q":"Aztreonam's spectrum is described as resembling which other class?","choices":["Macrolides","Glycopeptides","Nitroimidazoles","Aminoglycosides"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Both cover Gram-negatives including Pseudomonas aeruginosa and Enterobacteriaceae, with no Gram-positive or anaerobic activity. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"What happened to ephedra-containing herbal products?","choices":["They were reclassified as prescription only","They remain freely available","They were banned in 2004","They were moved behind the pharmacy counter"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 104"},{"q":"What is the risk of stopping clonidine abruptly?","choices":["Rebound hypertension","Rebound bronchospasm","Adrenal suppression","Rebound nasal congestion"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, which is why it is tapered rather than stopped. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 97"},{"q":"Which adverse effects limit flucytosine?","choices":["Nephrotoxicity and electrolyte wasting requiring hydration with normal saline","Visual disturbance in about 30 percent of patients","Tendon rupture and peripheral neuropathy","Bone marrow suppression, hepatotoxicity, gastrointestinal disturbance and rash"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and it is available orally only. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 101"},{"q":"Within what window must oseltamivir be started?","choices":["As soon as possible, within 48 hours of symptom onset","Within 7 days of symptom onset","Within the first 24 hours, as with acyclovir in chickenpox","At any point during the illness, with equal benefit"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Because it blocks release rather than killing virus already present, late treatment has little to act on. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 126"},{"q":"How does vancomycin inhibit cell wall synthesis, and how does this differ from a penicillin?","choices":["It inactivates the penicillin-binding protein by forming a covalent bond at its active site, exactly as a penicillin does","It inhibits the 50S ribosomal subunit and blocks transpeptidation","It disrupts the outer membrane by displacing magnesium and calcium ions","It binds the two D-alanine residues on the peptide to block cross-linking, rather than inactivating the cross-linking enzyme"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Penicillin mimics the substrate and disables the enzyme; vancomycin covers the substrate itself, which is why resistance is harder to develop. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"Which laboratory change does isotretinoin cause?","choices":["Increased serum lipids","Reduced serum calcium","Raised serum immunoglobulin E","Reduced white cell count"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and it sits alongside retinoid dermatitis, photophobia, arthralgia, headaches, alopecia and brittle nails. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"What happens to benzoyl peroxide after it penetrates the stratum corneum?","choices":["It is converted to salicylic acid, which is keratinolytic","It is converted to retinoic acid, which corrects keratinization","It remains unchanged in the skin and works purely by oxidising sebum within the follicle","It is converted to benzoic acid, which is active against Propionibacterium acnes"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. It crosses unchanged and is converted once through. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"Why is succinylcholine suited to rapid sequence intubation?","choices":["Its lack of any cardiovascular effect","Its reversibility with neostigmine","Its rapid onset and short duration of action","Its long duration, which covers the whole procedure"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, with the short duration owing to rapid breakdown by plasma enzyme. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 66"},{"q":"How are the neuromuscular blocking drugs described structurally?","choices":["Structural analogues of acetylcholine","As structural analogues of norepinephrine acting at adrenergic receptors","As enzymes that degrade acetylcholine at the endplate","As calcium channel blockers acting on the sarcoplasmic reticulum"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and both are used during surgery to produce complete muscle relaxation. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 61"},{"q":"Which hypertensive crisis is phentolamine used for?","choices":["Crisis in pre-eclampsia","Crisis from cocaine intoxication","Crisis in thyroid storm","Abrupt clonidine withdrawal"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, which is the rebound the clonidine slide warns about. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 110"},{"q":"What kind of infection are the allylamines used for?","choices":["Superficial dermatophyte infections","Systemic mycoses of the internal organs","Cryptococcal meningitis","Helminth infection"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Naftifine is topical and terbinafine is available orally and topically. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 112"},{"q":"How long does griseofulvin treatment take for scalp, fingernail and toenail infection?","choices":["Three months for all three sites","A single dose for the scalp, with topical therapy thereafter for nails","About one month for the scalp, six to nine months for fingernails, and up to twelve months for toenails","Approximately one week for the scalp, one month for the fingernails, and three months for the toenails"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The gradient follows how slowly each keratin structure grows out, and it is the counselling point that keeps patients adherent. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 110"},{"q":"How do levofloxacin and moxifloxacin differ in coverage?","choices":["Levofloxacin has Pseudomonas coverage; moxifloxacin does not","Moxifloxacin has Pseudomonas coverage; levofloxacin does not","Levofloxacin covers atypicals; moxifloxacin does not","Moxifloxacin requires renal adjustment; levofloxacin does not"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Both improve on ciprofloxacin's Gram-positive activity and both cover community-acquired pneumonia. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 77"},{"q":"Which organism does griseofulvin fail to cover, and what is the nature of its action?","choices":["It is not effective against dermatophytes, and it is fungicidal","It is not effective against Aspergillus, and it is fungicidal against Candida","It covers all fungi but only when given topically","It is not effective against Candida, and it is fungistatic rather than fungicidal"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. It is effective against numerous dermatophytes, which is a different target population entirely. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 111"},{"q":"Linezolid is the drug of choice for which infections, and what does it not cover?","choices":["Hospital-acquired and community-acquired methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus; it covers no Gram-negatives or anaerobes","Pseudomonal and multidrug-resistant Gram-negative infection; it covers no Gram-positive organisms and no atypicals","Anaerobic intra-abdominal infection; it does not cover aerobes","Atypical pneumonia; it does not cover Staphylococcus"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. It also covers multidrug-resistant pneumococcus and vancomycin-resistant Enterococcus. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 71"},{"q":"What is the rank order of potency at beta receptors?","choices":["Norepinephrine, then epinephrine, then isoproterenol","All three are equipotent at beta receptors","Isoproterenol, then epinephrine, then norepinephrine","Epinephrine, then norepinephrine, then isoproterenol"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The alpha order runs the other way, with epinephrine and norepinephrine far above isoproterenol. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 77"},{"q":"Through which receptor does epinephrine produce bronchodilation?","choices":["Beta-two","Beta-one","Alpha-one","Alpha-two"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, the same receptor albuterol targets selectively. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 84"},{"q":"What are dopamine's adverse effects?","choices":["Bronchoconstriction","Nausea, hypertension and arrhythmias","Tremor, restlessness and anxiety","Sedation and dry mouth"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 93"},{"q":"What is the anti-inflammatory action of tetracyclines in acne?","choices":["They stimulate macrophages to release tumour necrosis factor alpha","They chelate calcium ions, which prevents neutrophil and monocyte chemotaxis","They stabilise mast cell membranes and block histamine release","They inhibit the conversion of testosterone to dihydrotestosterone"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. That is an anti-inflammatory action separate from killing the organism. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"How are acne lesions divided?","choices":["Noninflammatory as open and closed comedones; inflammatory as papulopustular and nodular lesions","Noninflammatory as papules; inflammatory as comedones","Superficial as pustular; deep as comedonal","Acute disease as nodular and pustular lesions; chronic disease as open and closed comedones alone"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. That split is what decides whether topical or systemic therapy is chosen. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"Which renal adverse effect is characteristic of acyclovir, and how is it mitigated?","choices":["Its intravenous vehicle accumulates in renal failure","It crystallises in the renal tubule, so hydration and renal function must be maintained","It causes direct renal tubule damage requiring hydration with normal saline","It causes interstitial nephritis requiring corticosteroids"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Note how this differs from amphotericin B, which damages the tubule directly rather than crystallising in it. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 123"},{"q":"Compared with acyclovir, what makes ganciclovir the harder drug to tolerate?","choices":["Crystallisation within the renal tubule, requiring aggressive hydration throughout treatment","Severe infusion reactions requiring premedication","A disulfiram-like reaction on any alcohol exposure","Marked neutropenia and thrombocytopenia, with about a third of patients stopping intravenous treatment"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Acyclovir's bone marrow suppression is listed, but ganciclovir's is far more limiting. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 125"},{"q":"Why is pseudoephedrine kept behind the pharmacy counter?","choices":["It requires blood pressure monitoring","It was banned by the Food and Drug Administration","It can be converted to methamphetamine","It causes rebound congestion after three days"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, which is a regulatory rather than a pharmacological reason. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 104"},{"q":"Which carbapenem lacks Pseudomonas aeruginosa coverage?","choices":["Meropenem","Imipenem","Doripenem","Ertapenem"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The class covers Pseudomonas as a rule, and ertapenem is the stated exception. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"What are the two proposed actions of azelaic acid?","choices":["Inhibition of fungal cell wall formation and keratinolysis","Conversion to benzoic acid within the stratum corneum together with a direct comedolytic peeling effect","Inhibition of T cell activation and mast cell stabilisation","Antimicrobial activity and inhibition of the conversion of testosterone to dihydrotestosterone"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and is explicit that the mechanism is not fully understood. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"Once phosphorylated and incorporated into viral DNA, how does acyclovir stop replication?","choices":["It is a guanine analogue lacking the sugar moiety, so the chain cannot elongate past it","It cross-links the two strands of viral DNA so that they can no longer separate","It causes helical structure loss and strand breakage","It inhibits DNA gyrase and prevents supercoiling"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — chain termination by a missing sugar, which is a structural rather than an enzymatic block. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 121"},{"q":"Which beta-lactamase inhibitor is paired with piperacillin?","choices":["Sulbactam, marketed as Unasyn","Clavulanate, marketed as Augmentin","Avibactam, marketed as Avycaz","Tazobactam, marketed as Zosyn"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Sulbactam pairs with ampicillin as Unasyn, and clavulanate with amoxicillin as Augmentin. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"What is the mechanism of the benzimidazoles albendazole and mebendazole?","choices":["They inhibit ergosterol synthesis in the helminth membrane","They interact with parasite DNA to cause strand breakage","They inhibit formation of helminth microtubules and block glucose uptake, leading to parasite death","They release acetylcholine and inhibit cholinesterase, acting as a depolarizing neuromuscular blocker"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Note this sits outside the three drug classes covered — antibacterials, antivirals and antifungals. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 115"},{"q":"How does an alpha-two agonist such as clonidine lower blood pressure?","choices":["It depletes vesicular norepinephrine","It reduces central sympathetic outflow","It blocks alpha-one receptors on vascular smooth muscle","It blocks beta-one receptors in the heart"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, so an agonist produces vasodilation by damping sympathetic drive. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 97"},{"q":"Which is the major inhibitory transmitter in the brain?","choices":["Norepinephrine, which regulates normal brain processes","Gamma-aminobutyric acid","Glycine, the inhibitory transmitter of spinal cord neurons","Acetylcholine, which controls memory in brain neurons"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, while glycine is given as the inhibitory transmitter used by spinal cord neurons. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"Which two agents are allylamines, and how do they work?","choices":["Naftifine and terbinafine, which inhibit ergosterol production and prevent cell wall synthesis","Clotrimazole and miconazole, which inhibit fungal cytochrome P450","Nystatin and tolnaftate, which bind fungal membrane sterols directly and cause leakage of cell contents","Ciclopirox and sertaconazole, which block precursor uptake"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, with local irritation as the adverse reaction. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"Which atypical organisms do macrolides cover?","choices":["Bacteroides fragilis and Clostridium perfringens","Rickettsiae, Borrelia burgdorferi and Brucella","Legionella species, Mycoplasma pneumoniae, Chlamydophila pneumoniae and Chlamydia trachomatis","Pseudomonas aeruginosa, Acinetobacter species, Burkholderia cepacia and Stenotrophomonas maltophilia"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and this atypical coverage is what makes them a community-acquired pneumonia agent. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"Three prodrug and parent pairs appear in the antiviral section. Which pairing is correct?","choices":["None of these antivirals is a prodrug","Valacyclovir with acyclovir, famciclovir with penciclovir, and valganciclovir with ganciclovir","Valacyclovir with ganciclovir, famciclovir with acyclovir, and valganciclovir with penciclovir","Oseltamivir with acyclovir, valacyclovir with penciclovir, and famciclovir with ganciclovir"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. In each case the prodrug exists to solve poor oral absorption of the parent. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 124"},{"q":"Which gap in coverage does tolnaftate have?","choices":["It has no dermatophyte activity","It has no activity below the stratum corneum","It has no activity on the scalp","It has no Candida activity"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and nystatin is the agent pairs with candidal infection. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"Which indications are given for cefazolin?","choices":["Meningitis and neonatal sepsis","Complicated intra-abdominal infection with Bacteroides fragilis, anaerobic abscess and biliary sepsis","Surgical prophylaxis, methicillin-susceptible Staphylococcus aureus infection, and urinary tract infection","Neutropenic fever and hospital-acquired pneumonia"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Cephalexin covers a similar niche orally, aimed at cellulitis and urinary tract infection. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"How does atropine's cardiovascular effect vary with dose?","choices":["No cardiac effect, since its action is confined to secretions","Bradycardia at lower doses and tachycardia at higher doses","Tachycardia at lower doses and bradycardia at higher doses","Bradycardia at every dose"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and it is given during a code to treat bradycardia. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"What is the allylamine mechanism of action?","choices":["Binding ergosterol already present and forming membrane channels","Inhibition of glucan synthesis in the cell wall","Inhibition of squalene epoxidase, reducing ergosterol synthesis","Inhibition of lanosterol demethylase, reducing ergosterol synthesis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Same end point as the azoles, reached at an earlier step of the pathway. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 112"},{"q":"Which agents treat Alzheimer disease, and why?","choices":["Physostigmine, neostigmine and pyridostigmine, because they cross into the brain","Atropine, scopolamine and glycopyrrolate, because they reduce secretions","Pralidoxime and dantrolene, because they restore enzyme function","Donepezil, rivastigmine and galantamine"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and their common adverse effect is gastrointestinal distress. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"Clindamycin is the drug of choice for which situations?","choices":["Bacterial meningitis, cerebral abscess and ventriculitis requiring reliable central nervous system penetration, together with neurosurgical prophylaxis after craniotomy","Toxin-mediated disease, skin and soft tissue infection, osteomyelitis, surgical prophylaxis in penicillin allergy, and as part of intra-abdominal combination therapy","Pseudomonal pneumonia and febrile neutropenia","Urinary tract infection and pyelonephritis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The toxin-mediated indication follows from inhibiting bacterial protein synthesis and therefore toxin production. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 80"},{"q":"Which muscles does a nondepolarizing blocker paralyse first?","choices":["The muscles of the trunk and neck","All skeletal muscle simultaneously","The small muscles of the face and eye","The diaphragm and intercostal muscles"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and the diaphragm goes last, with recovery in reverse order. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 65"},{"q":"What distinguishes scopolamine from atropine?","choices":["Greater central action, preventing motion sickness","It has no central effects and acts purely peripherally","It stimulates muscarinic receptors rather than blocking them","It is a selective bladder agent"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, with sedation at lower doses and excitement at higher ones. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"An exacerbation needs a medium to high potency steroid. What next?","choices":["Add an oral corticosteroid for the same duration","Use it for one to two weeks, then switch to a lower potency","Continue it indefinitely to prevent relapse","Stop abruptly once the flare settles and use nothing further"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The step-down is the protocol rather than continuing at strength. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"What distinguishes tolnaftate's spectrum from the other topical antifungals?","choices":["It is effective against tinea unguium of the nails but against none of the other tineas","It covers Candida but no dermatophytes","It covers systemic mycoses when given orally","It is effective against tinea pedis, tinea cruris and tinea corporis, but not tinea unguium"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. It works by distorting hyphae and stunting mycelial growth, and is topical only. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 113"},{"q":"Which antifungal target exists in the fungal cell wall and has no mammalian counterpart?","choices":["Lanosterol demethylase, which the azole agents inhibit","Microtubules, which griseofulvin disrupts","1,3-beta-D-glucan, whose synthase the echinocandins inhibit","Ergosterol, which the polyenes bind"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Mammalian cells have no cell wall at all, which makes this the cleanest selective target among the antifungals. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 94"},{"q":"Which amphotericin B formulation is the conventional, non-lipid one?","choices":["Amphotericin B lipid complex, marketed as Abelcet","Liposomal amphotericin B, marketed as AmBisome","Amphotericin B colloidal suspension, marketed as Amphotec","Amphotericin B deoxycholate, marketed as Fungizone"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The three lipid alternatives are the lipid complex, the colloidal suspension and the liposomal form. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 97"},{"q":"Which muscarinic receptor subtypes are functionally characterised?","choices":["M2 and M4 only, the cardiac subtypes","M1, M2 and M3 only, of five subclasses","All five subclasses, M1 through M5","M1 and M2 only"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. M4 and M5 are listed as neuronal without further characterisation. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"Which organisms do tetracyclines cover well?","choices":["Anaerobes exclusively, with no aerobic activity","Atypicals and animal-borne organisms — Yersinia pestis, Brucella, Borrelia burgdorferi and rickettsiae","Pseudomonas aeruginosa, Clostridium difficile, Enterococcus faecium and Acinetobacter baumannii","Enterococcus and Listeria monocytogenes only"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Poor coverage is specified for Pseudomonas and Clostridium difficile. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 62"},{"q":"Which macrolide-containing regimen is named for Helicobacter pylori?","choices":["Prevpac — clarithromycin, amoxicillin and lansoprazole","Clarithromycin with ethambutol and rifabutin","Azithromycin with doxycycline","Erythromycin with metronidazole and omeprazole"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A macrolide, a penicillin and a proton pump inhibitor together. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"Which set matches the nicotinic effects?","choices":["Vasodilation, atony of the bladder, cycloplegia and xerostomia","Mydriasis, tachycardia, weakness, hypertension and fasciculations","Miosis, bradycardia, bronchospasm, lacrimation and salivation","Sedation, hyperthermia, flushing, dry mouth and urinary retention"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and note this set moves the pupil and heart in the opposite direction to the muscarinic set. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"What is pyridostigmine's clinical role?","choices":["Reactivating acetylcholinesterase after organophosphate exposure","Reducing intraocular pressure in glaucoma","Chronic management of myasthenia gravis","Diagnosis of myasthenia gravis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, as distinct from edrophonium's diagnostic role. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"Which herpesviruses are grouped as targets for antiviral therapy?","choices":["Herpes simplex and influenza A only","Cytomegalovirus and human immunodeficiency virus","Varicella-zoster and hepatitis B","Herpes simplex, varicella-zoster and cytomegalovirus"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the first two share the acyclovir family, while cytomegalovirus needs ganciclovir. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 119"},{"q":"From which plants is atropine derived?","choices":["Ephedra and willow bark","Deadly nightshade, jimson weed and mandrake","Foxglove and lily of the valley","Opium poppy and coca"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and scopolamine comes from the same family. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"What enzyme do the imidazoles and triazoles inhibit?","choices":["1,3-beta-D-glucan synthase in the cell wall","Thymidylate synthase, blocking DNA synthesis","Fungal cytochrome P450 14-alpha-demethylase, which converts lanosterol to ergosterol","Squalene epoxidase, which acts early in the fungal ergosterol synthesis pathway"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Because the target is a cytochrome P450 enzyme, this class interferes with human cytochrome P450 enzymes too, which is the source of its drug interactions. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 102"},{"q":"Which non-neurologic adverse effects are listed for acyclovir?","choices":["Disulfiram-like reaction with ethanol","Nausea, vomiting, rash and bone marrow suppression","Neutropenia and thrombocytopenia as the dominant effects","Photosensitivity and tooth discoloration"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, with hydration and renal function to be maintained because of tubular crystallisation. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 123"},{"q":"Why do the noncatecholamines have longer half-lives?","choices":["They are stored in vesicles and released over hours","They are excreted unchanged by the kidney","They are not substrates for the inactivating enzymes","They are protein bound and released slowly from plasma"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and their greater lipid solubility also increases central penetration. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 80"}]};
